WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.420
AI logs can become a privacy risk very quickly.

2
00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:17.480
Prompts may include customer data, personal information, project secrets, source excerpts, internal file paths, API keys, credentials pasted by mistake, or sensitive business context.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.700
Outputs may repeat or transform that information.

4
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:27.040
Tool arguments and retrieved chunks can add even more data to telemetry if logging is careless.

5
00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.360
Safe logging starts with structured events.

6
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:44.380
Instead of recording full prompts and responses by default, log fields such as workflow name, model version, request category, status, latency, error class, retrieval count, tool status, and review state.

7
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:50.480
These fields support operations without turning routine logs into a shadow copy of the AI workload.

8
00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:54.280
Redaction and masking should be designed deliberately.

9
00:00:54.380 --> 00:01:08.620
Secrets, tokens, personal data, customer identifiers, file paths, tenant identifiers, and sensitive source references may need to be removed, hashed, generalized, or stored only in restricted investigation records.

10
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.400
Redaction should be tested because AI workflows often combine data from several places.

11
00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.560
Separate routine logging from debug logging.

12
00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:26.620
Debug logs may be useful during development or incident response, but they should have clear controls, short retention, restricted access, and a way to turn them off.

13
00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.160
Production-style logs should usually be more conservative.

14
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.560
Finally, treat logs as data.

15
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:38.940
They need access control, retention rules, review, and deletion paths.

16
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:46.480
A sovereign AI system is not truly private if its telemetry quietly becomes the largest uncontrolled data store in the environment.
